## 9. Design Decisions

### [DD-CAN-NATIVE-PER-TIER] Drop python-can; go native per tier (RATIFIED IN DESIGN — recorded here)

- decision: drop `python-can` (LGPL-3.0) and go NATIVE PER TIER — stdlib SocketCAN on Linux/RPi ([CXC-CAN-SOCKETCAN]), ESP-IDF TWAI (Apache-2.0) on ESP32/CYD ([CXC-CAN-TWAI]). There is NO shared CAN library; the tiers meet at the ComposeBus namespace contract ([CXC-CAN-CONTRACT]), and both readers are thin producers behind it.
- rationale: `python-can` earned nothing but an LGPL obligation — `bridge.py` used only raw `socketcan` frames, which is ~20 lines of stdlib. Dropping it is a dependency REDUCTION and a license WIN ([CXC-CAN-LICENSE]), taking the strongest option the MIT-readiness assessment itself named. A shared library is not merely unnecessary — it is IMPOSSIBLE: neither python-can nor SocketCAN exists on the ESP32, whose CAN controller is on-chip TWAI silicon with its own driver. The namespace contract is the only possible unifier, and it is the better one (reader board and display board decouple over the bus).
- alternatives: (a) keep python-can, isolated + "keep-optional-noted" (the MIT assessment's fallback) — rejected: retains the copyleft caution and a dependency that earns nothing; (b) attempt a shared CAN abstraction across tiers — rejected: physically impossible (SocketCAN != TWAI silicon), so any "shared" layer is two reimplementations with a shared name, i.e. exactly the two-evaluators-one-golden model ([CXC-CAN-DECODER]) minus the honesty.
- invalidating_assumption: that a shared CAN library is worth an LGPL dep. FALSIFIED once the ESP32 tier proves no shared library is even possible — SocketCAN and TWAI are different silicon with different drivers, so the "shared library" premise cannot hold across the two tiers this platform must serve.
- status: accepted (ratified in `docs/architecture/cross-tier-can-design.md`; recorded here for the graph).
- change: "Cross-tier CAN drops python-can (LGPL-3.0) in favor of native-per-tier controllers (stdlib SocketCAN on Linux, ESP-IDF TWAI on ESP32); the unifier moves from a shared library to a ComposeBus namespace contract."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "python-can (LGPL-3.0) was the CAN access layer for services/can_mqtt_bridge/bridge.py, listed by the MIT-readiness assessment (§2b/§2e/checklist P1-5) as the one copyleft dep in the publishable tree, with a keep-optional-noted fallback."
  - reasoning: "bridge.py used only raw socketcan frames — a ~20-line stdlib socket wrapper — so python-can added an LGPL obligation for zero unique capability. Native-per-tier eliminates the obligation AND reduces the dependency count, taking the stronger reimplement-over-SocketCAN option the assessment already named."
  - rejected_alternative: "Keep python-can isolated and noted (the assessment's fallback), or attempt a single shared CAN library across both tiers."
  - counter_argument: "A shared CAN library would give one code path across tiers and less duplication."
  - counter_response: "A shared library is impossible here: the ESP32 has no SocketCAN and no python-can — its controller is on-chip TWAI silicon with its own C driver. Any 'shared' layer would be two reimplementations behind one name; the honest model is one declarative decoder map with two evaluators crosschecked against one golden ([CXC-CAN-DECODER]), which is cleaner than a false-shared library."
  - invalidating_assumption: "That a shared CAN library is worth an LGPL dep — false once the ESP32 tier proves no shared library is even possible (SocketCAN != TWAI silicon)."
  - implication: "The unifier is the namespace contract ([CXC-CAN-CONTRACT]); readers are interchangeable producers; the MIT-readiness caution disappears ([CXC-CAN-LICENSE], strike P1-5); reader board and display board decouple over the bus."
- see-also: [CXC-CAN-CONTRACT], [CXC-CAN-LICENSE], [CXC-CAN-DECODER]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CAN]

### [DD-CAN-SPEC-HOME] Spec home — one cohesive .lts doc now; contract MAY relocate beside [BP-MSG-CAN] on migration (FLAGGED)

- decision: where the namespace contract and the reader/decoder mechanics live. The design recommended the namespace contract live BESIDE the bus protocol (a `[BP-CAN-FOREIGN]` section under `spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd`) with the reader/decoder mechanics in their own doc.
- chosen: author ONE cohesive `.lts` document (this file, `corpora/platform-specs/52-composeux-cross-tier-can.lts`) capturing BOTH the contract (§1, [CXC-CAN-CONTRACT] realizing the `[BP-CAN-FOREIGN]` concept) AND the reader/decoder mechanics (§2-§7), in the `.lts` corpus, referencing [BP-MSG-CAN] / [BP-CAN-ID] as registered-prefix cross-doc refs.
- rationale: the bus-protocol spec is still `.smd` (unmigrated); this corpus authors `.lts`, and this agent authors `.lts`. Splitting the contract INTO the `.smd` would fork the format from a `.lts`-authoring role and edit an unmigrated corpus. The split's benefit (contract frozen beside the protocol) is premised on the contract living in an EXISTING doc — moot while the protocol is `.smd`. The contract + mechanics are tightly coupled and both new, so one cohesive, ratify-once doc is cleaner. The contract node ([CXC-CAN-CONTRACT]) is demarcated so it can relocate beside [BP-MSG-CAN] if/when the bus protocol migrates to `.lts`.
- alternatives: (a) edit the `.smd` bus protocol to add a `[BP-CAN-FOREIGN]` section (rejected: forks the format from a `.lts` role, touches an unmigrated corpus); (b) split into two new `.lts` docs, contract + mechanics (rejected: the split's rationale evaporates when the contract cannot sit beside the `.smd` protocol; two new docs to ratify instead of one, for no isolation gain).
- invalidating_assumption: that the bus protocol remains `.smd`. If it migrates to `.lts`, [CXC-CAN-CONTRACT] SHOULD be revisited for relocation beside [BP-MSG-CAN], leaving the reader/decoder mechanics here.
- status: proposed — flagged for the architect to ratify (home + the relocation-on-migration policy).
- see-also: [CXC-CAN-CONTRACT], [BP-MSG-CAN]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CAN]

### [DD-CAN-BOARD] The ESP32 board choice — CYD 2-pin budget vs a standalone ESP32/S3 CAN node (FLAGGED)

- decision: whether the TWAI CAN reader lives on the CYD (consuming 2 of its 3 free P3 GPIOs) or on a standalone bare-ESP32 / qualia-S3 node.
- chosen (recommended, pending ratify): a STANDALONE ESP32/S3 CAN reader publishing `fiat/*`, so the display board (CYD or RPi) stays a pure subscriber — maximal use of the contract's reader/display decoupling ([CXC-CAN-CONTRACT]). The CYD-hosted option remains viable for a fixed CAN dash where the two-pin budget is acceptable.
- rationale: the CYD CAN host CAN (TWAI_TX=21, TWAI_RX=35, GPIO35 input-only forcing RX), but it consumes 2 of 3 free pins with everything else committed. A standalone node has GPIO headroom (transceiver enable, bus-off LED, a second bus) and the contract already decouples reader board from display board — so putting CAN on its own board costs nothing at the consumer and buys pin headroom.
- alternatives: (a) CYD-hosted CAN (viable + cheapest for a permanent small-panel dash; tight on pins); (b) qualia-S3-hosted CAN (headroom, but pairs CAN to the heavier render tier when a bare ESP32 devkit suffices for a reader-only node).
- invalidating_assumption: that the CAN node needs more than two GPIOs OR that the reader and display want to be separate boards. If the target is strictly a fixed two-gauge dash on one CYD, the CYD-hosted option is acceptable and cheapest.
- status: proposed — flagged for the architect to ratify at Phase 2.
- see-also: [CXC-CAN-BOARD], [CXC-CAN-TWAI]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CAN]

